window.docContents[162].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Homomorphism-of-quiver-representations/#Chapter2___Definition2___8___10":{"contents":"Definition 2.8.10. Let (V_i, x_h) and (W_i, y_h) be representations of the quiver Q. A homomorphism \\varphi : (V_i) \\longrightarrow (W_i) of quiver representations is a collection of maps \\varphi_i : V_i \\longrightarrow W_i such that y_h \\circ \\varphi_{h'} = \\varphi_{h''} \\circ x_h for all h \\in E.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.8. Quivers\u0009Homomorphism of quiver representations","header":"Homomorphism of quiver representations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___8___-Quivers/Homomorphism-of-quiver-representations/#Chapter2___Definition2___8___10"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Representations-of-finite-abelian-groups-and-dual-groups/#Chapter4___Example4___3_FiniteAbelianGroups":{"contents":"(1) Finite abelian groups G = \\mathbb{Z}_{n_1} \\times \\cdots \\times \\mathbb{Z}_{n_k}. Let G^\\vee be the set of irreducible representations of G. Every element of G forms a conjugacy class, so |G^\\vee| = |G|. Recall that all irreducible representations over \\mathbb{C} (and algebraically closed fields in general) of commutative algebras and groups are 1-dimensional. Thus, G^\\vee is an abelian group: if \\rho_1, \\rho_2 : G \\to \\mathbb{C}^\\times are irreducible representations, then so are the representations \\rho_1(g)\\rho_2(g) and \\rho_1(g)^{-1}. The group G^\\vee is called the dual group or character group of G.\n\nFor given n \\geq 1, define \\rho : \\mathbb{Z}_n \\to \\mathbb{C}^\\times by \\rho(m) = e^{2\\pi i m/n}. Then \\mathbb{Z}_n^\\vee = \\{\\rho^k : k = 0, \\ldots, n - 1\\}, so \\mathbb{Z}_n^\\vee \\cong \\mathbb{Z}_n. In general,\n\n(G_1 \\times G_2 \\times \\cdots \\times G_n)^\\vee = G_1^\\vee \\times G_2^\\vee \\times \\cdots \\times G_n^\\vee,\nso G^\\vee \\cong G for any finite abelian group G. This isomorphism is, however, noncanonical: the particular decomposition of G as \\mathbb{Z}_{n_1} \\times \\cdots \\times \\mathbb{Z}_{n_k} is not unique as far as which elements of G correspond to \\mathbb{Z}_{n_1}, etc., is concerned. On the other hand, G \\cong (G^\\vee)^\\vee is a canonical isomorphism, given by \\varphi : G \\to (G^\\vee)^\\vee, where \\varphi(g)(\\chi) = \\chi(g).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples\u0009Representations of finite abelian groups and dual groups","header":"Representations of finite abelian groups and dual groups","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Representations-of-finite-abelian-groups-and-dual-groups/#Chapter4___Example4___3_FiniteAbelianGroups"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Section-4___3___-Examples/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___3":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples\u0009Section 4.3: Examples","header":"Section 4.3: Examples","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Section-4___3___-Examples/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___3"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Decomposition-of-U_lambda-in-terms-of-V_mu-with-Kostka-numbers/#Chapter5___Proposition5___14___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.14. Induced representations for $S_n$\u0009Decomposition of U_lambda in terms of V_mu with Kostka numbers\u0009Decomposition of U_lambda in terms of V_mu with Kostka numbers","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___14___-Induced-representations-for-___S_n___/Decomposition-of-U_lambda-in-terms-of-V_mu-with-Kostka-numbers/#Chapter5___Proposition5___14___1___formalization"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___9___-Problems/Problem-6___9___2___-E8-lattice-and-root-systems/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___9___-Problems--Problem-6___9___2___-E8-lattice-and-root-systems--Problem-6___9___2___-E8-lattice-and-root-systems--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary set of rational vectors indexed by eight coordinates.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nA second auxiliary set of rational vectors with eight coordinates.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe transformed first auxiliary vector set has cardinality seventy-two.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe transformed second auxiliary vector set has cardinality one hundred twenty-six.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe transformed third auxiliary vector set has cardinality two hundred forty.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe third auxiliary set consists exactly of integer linear combinations of the indexed rational vectors.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe eight-vector construction has the stated root-set, membership, matrix, and permuted\nadjacency properties.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Section6.9_heading; role=supporting]\n\nThe auxiliary set associated with the eight-vector data satisfies the crystallographic\nroot-set predicate.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe integer pairing matrix of the seven-vector family agrees entrywise with the given\nadjacency matrix.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe integer pairing matrix of the six-vector family agrees entrywise with the given\nadjacency matrix.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Problem6.9.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.9. Problems\u0009Problem 6.9.2: E8 lattice and root systems\u0009Problem 6.9.2: E8 lattice and root systems\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___9___-Problems/Problem-6___9___2___-E8-lattice-and-root-systems/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___9___-Problems--Problem-6___9___2___-E8-lattice-and-root-systems--Problem-6___9___2___-E8-lattice-and-root-systems--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/Drawbacks-of-Definition-7___7___1_SEMI_-Morita-equivalence/#Chapter7___Remark7___7___4___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.7. Abelian categories\u0009Drawbacks of Definition 7.7.1; Morita equivalence\u0009Drawbacks of Definition 7.7.1; Morita equivalence","header":"Formalization","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___7___-Abelian-categories/Drawbacks-of-Definition-7___7___1_SEMI_-Morita-equivalence/#Chapter7___Remark7___7___4___formalization"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Simple-modules___-projective-modules___-and-Cartan-matrix-of-a-specific-algebra/#Chapter9___Problem9___3___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.3. The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra\u0009Simple modules, projective modules, and Cartan matrix of a specific algebra\u0009Simple modules, projective modules, and Cartan matrix of a specific algebra","header":"Formalization","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Simple-modules___-projective-modules___-and-Cartan-matrix-of-a-specific-algebra/#Chapter9___Problem9___3___2___formalization"}});